{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1033862-introducing-new-players-seeking-advice","id":1033862,"title":"Introducing New Players - Seeking Advice","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-17T20:28:34-08:00","id":1033862,"title":"Introducing New Players - Seeking Advice","content":"So in a few weeks, after one of my groups current campaign comes to an end (completely different system), I plan to try introducing them to Mage. I'm looking for a bit of advice on the best way to ease them into the game. As far as I know, all but one of the groups tabletop experience begins and ends with D&amp;D, and I'm not sure they've even played much of that. With that in mind, I'm not sure how I want to ease them into the game. I know from experience as well as watching one of my roommates (who is the only one in my group I know is fairly experienced when it comes to tabletop gaming) work his way through it that the mage book is a ridiculously dense read. I don't really expect the rest to get through such a mighty tome without getting their feet wet with some actual gameplay. <br /> <br /> So, my question for you all is this: How do I ease them into this without completely drowning them in unfamiliar mechanics and lore? How do I break apart this game in order to feed it to them in manageable bite-sized pieces?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-17T20:59:37-08:00","id":1033868,"title":"","content":"Some people like the shift from human-to-whatever. I like it in some games but I think there's a lot more to get across in mage, with the Awakening.<br /> <br /> While the mage book can be a dense read, the important part for players is the beginning, with the Paths and Orders, which can help them choose the characters they want. The rest of the world can be explained in-character during play. I found the Open Development blogs to be really good at getting a lot of information across in a short amount of words.<br /> <br /> One way you could do it might be to have the characters start off as ready-made mages who would die or be replaced after the introduction. Based on what they saw of those characters they could make more informed choices about what they might be like in play.<br /> <br /> Another way that might help could be to narrow it down, put something off limits, like the Free Council. If you have an idea for a story you can suggest that certain Paths might not be appropriate for it. This is probably the easiest way but also can be a harsh way to run things, in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T00:37:34-08:00","id":1033910,"title":"","content":"The easiest way for me to introduce the players to a new game is to start off explaining the game and what it can do. Get their imagination going and then start introducing them to the rules. Explain it in similarities to other games that you feel they may understand and then have sample character ready. and like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> said use those sample character in a sample game where they start to wrap their minds around how to play. The have a brainstorming session where you start introducing the game rules starting with character generation moving into the more advanced stuff. Suggest a mini story line where they can try different stuff, letting them know that if they like the system a full campaign will start where the characters can be made with the full extent of the rules. If they like that game there will start reading the book on their own and start asking questions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12802-hardwire99","name":"Hardwire99","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12802_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-18T01:58:48-08:00","id":1033922,"title":"","content":"I start other way around - I run with whole Order introduction Arc in Chronicle. You start a stories with Sleepers or just Awakened. Then comes Orders mentors showing and explaining basics of magic, adding their Order dogma spin. If character do not agree with NPCs Order dogma, let NPC send them to others, known to her NPC from others Orders, as Favors paying. PCs will have variety of Orders options, players will be slowly intruducted to Pentacle lore and you will all have story or two before really starting the main plot thing.<br /> <br /> As to how exactly to make this chronicle structer, I have two examples:<ul><li><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/929782-tasks-for-novice-mages\">My Boston game</a>, run with this schema in mind - my players, after almost month of being Awakened, are finally ending their Apprenticeship in Orders, becoming proper cabal. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Linked topic have many ideas that Orders Apprentices can do and are requested in Orders their train for.</li> <li>I can recommend last year TV series <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QS_20JPaEnA\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Magicians</b></a>, as first season starts with 'introduction in to Mysterium Academy and Nameless Order', but then goes in to full blown <b>Changeling</b> crossover - still, there is marking of Changeling-like storyline from the beginning. It can nicely show how to introduce players to <b>Mage</b> and run chronicle with Apprenticeship storyline in mind. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></li> <li>From practical advises, I split mage template in to parts that PCs got for free after some time in game ( most notable few days per each ), as Orders are giving them all shine toys they give new Awakened joining them. We started with characters having Gnosis 1 and Arcana spread of 2,1,1 of their chosen Path and from there character got one 'Order trening benefit' after each few days of downtime:<ul><li>High Speech Merit</li> <li>+1 Arcana dot ( gotten twice )</li> <li>+1 Occult Skill dot</li> <li>3 free Rotes</li> <li>Order Status 1 Merit</li> </ul></li> </ul>This made Apprenticeship memorable and give time for players to acclimate to <b>Mage</b> 2E mechanics. Now my players are at understanding Rotes and Praxis in the system. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I can also advise giving one extra Obsession slot for cabal/chronicle main Mystery, as only 1 slot on Gnosis 1 can be too low. It was not enough for my two players groups. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-18-2017, 05:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:07:10-08:00","id":1037919,"title":"","content":"I'm mostly happy with what I did for my new game. My players are a parent and his 9 and 11 year old kids.<br /> <br /> Here is my outline of how I explained the game:<br /> <br /> - Reality (Phenomenal vs. Supernal)<br /> - The Exarchs/Fall (Fallen World, Abyss)<br /> - Sleepers/Quiescence<br /> - Oracles/Watchtowers<br /> - Awakening/Paths<br /> - Character Creation (Paths, Orders, etc...)<br /> - Setting selection (they chose Boston)<br /> - Went through a list of things you can do in the game and gauged interest (Politics, Battling Seers, Hunting for Artifacts etc...)<br /> <br /> As they came up in play:<br /> <br /> - Mage Sight &amp; Mana<br /> - Spells (Imagos, High Speech, Yantras, Spell Factors, Paradox, Reach, Creative Thaumaturgy Practices)<br /> <br /> The spell stuff rolled out over several spell castings to keep things as simple as possible.<br /> <br /> We skipped Rotes and Praxis at character creation and they are picking those as we play and they understand what they need more. So far after two sessions only two characters have each chosen one Rote each (Ward and Knit). I might skip Nimbus for a while as well.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1470-johnny-awesome","name":"Johnny Awesome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"447"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T14:16:33-08:00","id":1038570,"title":"","content":"Really good responses. As our current game begins to wind itself down I'll start trying to soften them up with bits and pieces of information. I'll see how they take to that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T14:58:51-08:00","id":1038598,"title":"","content":"My first time running Mage was under the 1st edition rules. I know my group pretty well and what I did was propose to them a modern horror/supernatural game where they would play agents of a secret government agency called Bureau 13 ( stole the name from an old RPG title). They never played any WW games before so I used that to get them familiar with the base system. I ran the game this way for several weeks. As they investigated the weird and supernatural they would get close to uncovering more, but I purposely had an NPC that would roadblock them. &quot;We'll handle it from here..&quot; etc...<br /> <br /> The NPC was actually a Mage that would become their mentor. Every time they'd complete a case this guy would be there. So finally came the night I had them do a group awakening. I took them aside and told them what strange things were happening we walked through the awakening. At the end of that session there was the NPC that they'd continually run into welcoming them to their new lives as mages. Now finally they were the people &quot;in the know&quot; that could tell others to step back while they handled things.That Mage game blossomed into a 3-4 year game. To this day they talk about how it was one of the best we've done and even the way I handled the switch from mortals to mages was great.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12662,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12662-bestial-warlust","user":"bestial Warlust","date":". 01-26-2017, 06:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12662-bestial-warlust","name":"bestial Warlust","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}